interface.longDescription:
DoubleTaxation (doubletaxation.app) tells an individual which country they are a tax resident of, and where their income is taxed.

ChatGPT asks the user the same questions as the website, one at a time: permanent home, family, centre of vital interests, days of presence, citizenship. The answers go to a rules engine, which computes the result. The result is not generated by the model.

The result includes:
• conclusion and a reliability score;
• how the domestic law of each country treats the person;
• the treaty tie-breaker (Art. 4) and any conflict between the two laws;
• which taxes apply in each country, in plain language;
• legal basis with links to the statutes and treaty texts, plus relevant court decisions;
• optional per-income rules (salary, self-employment, pensions, dividends, interest, royalties, capital gains, rental income and more): which state may tax it, treaty rate limits, the method of relief, and an illustrative calculation;
• a link that opens the same result on doubletaxation.app, where it can be saved as a PDF.

A separate tool shows the tax burden on a salary in 31 countries (OECD Taxing Wages method), including special tax regimes for individuals.

After the result, the assistant can show the next steps: how to get a certificate of residence, filing obligations as a resident and as a non-resident, what to do when leaving a country, how each country treats the year of the move, and whether an exit tax applies.

Other tools, for 31 countries:
• day counter: the user lists their trips, and the tool counts days of presence in each country under that country's own counting rule, shows the day threshold and the date it is reached, and checks the Schengen 90/180 rule (trips are not stored);
• special tax regimes for individuals (newcomers, employees, the self-employed, investors, pensioners): who qualifies, the benefit, duration, how to apply, limits and the legal basis;
• exit tax on individuals: who pays, on what, deferral or relief;
• business forms: tax and contributions on a business profit as a sole proprietor under each tax system or through one's own company.

For general and theoretical questions (what tax residence is, where it comes from and why it exists, how it differs from citizenship, tax sovereignty and jurisdiction, offshore zones, the legal status of a taxpayer, the history of taxing individuals), the assistant searches about 900 questions and answers from the monograph by the service's legal expert, Yevhen Marynchak, "The Financial-Legal Link Between an Individual and the State" (2nd edition, 2020), explains them in the user's language and points to the section and page of the original.

Coverage: 29 versions, each written for taxpayers of one home country and in its language: Ukraine, United States, Poland, Czechia, Romania, Hungary, Türkiye, China, India, Japan, Bangladesh, Pakistan, Germany, Austria, France, Spain, Portugal, Italy, Malaysia, Singapore, Taiwan, Sweden, Brazil, Argentina, Georgia, Mexico, Indonesia, United Kingdom, United Arab Emirates (Arabic). Each version checks the home country against 31 other countries.

Limitations: individuals only (not companies); tax residence for 32 countries, tax burden, regimes and business forms for 31; tax years 2024–2026. All tools are read-only. No account is needed. Answers are processed in memory only and are never stored or logged.

The result is general information, not legal or tax advice.
